<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<ScrollView xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:layout_width="match_parent"
    android:layout_height="match_parent"
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    android:padding="16dp"
    tools:context="com.example.mobile_computing.fileTransfer">

    <LinearLayout
        android:layout_width="match_parent"
        android:layout_height="wrap_content"
        android:orientation="vertical">

        <!-- Title Section -->
        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="Bluetooth Device Recognition"
            android:textSize="24sp"
            android:textStyle="bold"
            android:paddingBottom="8dp"/>

        <!-- Bluetooth Address -->
        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="• Bluetooth Address (BD_ADDR):"
            android:textSize="18sp"
            android:textStyle="bold"
            android:paddingTop="8dp"/>

        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="  • Each Bluetooth device has a unique 48-bit identifier known as the Bluetooth Device Address (BD_ADDR).\n  • This address is assigned at the factory and helps distinguish each device in the network.\n  • The BD_ADDR functions similarly to a MAC address in Wi-Fi."/>

        <View
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="1dp"
            android:background="#CCCCCC"
            android:layout_marginVertical="16dp"/>

        <!-- Inquiry and Scanning -->
        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="• Inquiry and Scanning:"
            android:textSize="18sp"
            android:textStyle="bold"
            android:paddingTop="8dp"/>

        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="  • When a device wants to connect, it initiates an inquiry process to discover nearby Bluetooth devices.\n  • Other devices respond with their Bluetooth addresses and, in some cases, their names.\n  • Inquiry is especially important for pairing new devices and establishing a list of nearby available connections."/>

        <View
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="1dp"
            android:background="#CCCCCC"
            android:layout_marginVertical="16dp"/>

        <!-- Device Name -->
        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="• Device Name (Friendly Name):"
            android:textSize="18sp"
            android:textStyle="bold"
            android:paddingTop="8dp"/>

        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="  • Each Bluetooth device has a 'friendly name' that is visible to other devices.\n  • This helps users identify devices by names rather than long addresses during pairing.\n  • The name is often customizable by the user, aiding easy recognition."/>

        <View
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="1dp"
            android:background="#CCCCCC"
            android:layout_marginVertical="16dp"/>

        <!-- Pairing and Bonding -->
        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="• Pairing and Bonding:"
            android:textSize="18sp"
            android:textStyle="bold"
            android:paddingTop="8dp"/>

        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="  • To establish a trusted connection, devices must go through a pairing process.\n  • During pairing, devices exchange security keys and create a bonded relationship for future connections.\n  • This is done using authentication protocols like PIN codes, passkeys, or simple secure pairing (SSP) methods."/>

        <View
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="1dp"
            android:background="#CCCCCC"
            android:layout_marginVertical="16dp"/>

        <!-- Link Layer Connections -->
        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="• Link Layer Connections:"
            android:textSize="18sp"
            android:textStyle="bold"
            android:paddingTop="8dp"/>

        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="  • After identification and pairing, the devices establish a Link Layer connection.\n  • A unique link key, generated during pairing, secures the communication.\n  • The Link Layer uses the BD_ADDR to ensure that data packets are delivered to the correct device."/>

        <View
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="1dp"
            android:background="#CCCCCC"
            android:layout_marginVertical="16dp"/>

        <!-- Profiles and Services -->
        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="• Profiles and Services:"
            android:textSize="18sp"
            android:textStyle="bold"
            android:paddingTop="8dp"/>

        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="  • Bluetooth devices also identify each other based on the profiles and services they offer, such as audio, file transfer, or health device profiles.\n  • This allows devices to automatically recognize compatible features and functionalities in each other."/>

        <View
            android:layout_width="match_parent"
            android:layout_height="1dp"
            android:background="#CCCCCC"
            android:layout_marginVertical="16dp"/>

        <!-- Role Assignments -->
        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="• Role Assignments:"
            android:textSize="18sp"
            android:textStyle="bold"
            android:paddingTop="8dp"/>

        <TextView
            android:layout_width="wrap_content"
            android:layout_height="wrap_content"
            android:text="  • In a Bluetooth connection, devices take on specific roles, like 'master' and 'slave' (or 'central' and 'peripheral' in Bluetooth Low Energy).\n  • The master device manages the connection parameters, while the slave device responds to master’s instructions.\n  • This role assignment helps organize and coordinate data transfer within a Bluetooth network."/>

    </LinearLayout>
</ScrollView>
